1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Technicians often arrive at an unfamiliar plant without knowing where the equipment is,

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
what's wrong with it, or how to reach it. That uncertainty costs time before work even begins.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Meet Industrial X-Ray. Using Valmet's 3D scan and equipment records, we build an interactive copy of the facility,

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,000
so technicians can explore, locate assets and prepare before they travel.

5
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:35,000
From the scan we segment 676 object candidates — and show honestly how many are confidently identified.

6
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Here we ask about pump P-005. The copilot gathers telemetry, alarms and work orders from the synthetic workbook:

7
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:50,000
likely fouling, with every source cited.

8
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:58,000
We dispatch our simulated inspection robot and switch to its camera — rendered from the scan, not a live feed.

9
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:05,000
When we block the aisle, it calculates a new route.

10
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:13,000
It stops in front of the pump and faces it. We open an exploded teaching model right inside the scene to understand the components.

11
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Close it, and we're exactly where we were.

12
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:28,000
In Plan mode we move the pallet truck out of the way.

13
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Collisions, clearance and the robot's route update instantly.

14
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Industrial X-Ray brings spatial understanding, navigation, diagnostic evidence and training into one workflow,

15
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:50,000
so technicians arrive better prepared. Today's data is synthetic.

16
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:00,000
A pilot would measure the time saved, and could later connect — read-only — to systems such as Valmet DNAe.

